\title{The crossing-number formula: statement and assembly}
\taxon{Theorem}
\meta{status}{proof-account}
\meta{dependencies}{[Complete bipartite graphs](cn-0002); [Crossing number](cn-0005); [Zarankiewicz number](cn-0006); [Odd lower bound](cn-0035); [Upper bound](cn-0036); [Even reduction](cn-0037)}
\meta{provenance}{[OpenAI, 23 September 2026](cn-0042), Theorem 1.1 and Section 5; reconstructed as a reading account on 9 October 2026. [Lean source: Main](/source-material/openai-math/lean/OAI/Combinatorics/Crossing/Main.lean).}

\p{\strong{Statement.} For all positive integers #{m,n}, the [ordinary crossing number](cn-0005) of [#{K_{m,n}}](cn-0002) is its [Zarankiewicz number](cn-0006):}

##{\operatorname{cr}(K_{m,n})=d_m d_n.}

\p{This is Theorem 1.1 of [the paper](cn-0042). The notes below reconstruct its proof. The label “proof-account” records that role; it does not assert that the accompanying Lean code was compiled in this workspace. See [the verification record](cn-0039).}

\p{\strong{Proof.} The [balanced-axis construction](cn-0036) gives the upper bound. If either part has at most two vertices, the claimed value is zero and nonnegativity gives equality. For odd #{n\geq3}, [the odd lower bound](cn-0035) proves the reverse inequality by converting signed intersections into a rank sum. For even #{n>2}, [vertex deletion](cn-0037) reduces the lower bound to the odd case. These cases exhaust the positive integers.}

\p{The [proof introduction](cn-0070) explains the subspace inequality, the matrix sizes and star indices, and the conversion from ranks to crossing counts. The [guided account](cn-0001) includes the final proof assembly in full.}
